  "_doc": {
    "what": "The GICS sub-industry NAMES the authoritative S&P feed uses. Not a classification of anything \u2014 a VOCABULARY, used to tell a canonical GICS sub-industry from a vendor's own label.",
    "why": "gics_classifier resolves a label per ticker; universe_builder then GROUPS BY THAT STRING. A vendor label that no alias canonicalises therefore creates its own group, which is below rotation.min_candidates and can never be eligible at any composite \u2014 the affected names are quarantined from the peers they should compete against, and those peers' groups are thinner than they should be. The failure is silent: a phantom group looks exactly like a real small group. This file is what makes it detectable.",
    "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/datasets/s-and-p-500-companies/main/data/constituents.csv",
    "derivation": "distinct values of the CSV's 'GICS Sub-Industry' column, as consumed by UniverseSource.sp500_gics_map",
    "as_of": "2026-08-10T18:40:32.105654+00:00",
    "constituents_covered": 503,
    "incomplete_deliberately": "These are the sub-industries with at least one S&P 500 constituent \u2014 NOT the full GICS taxonomy. A real GICS sub-industry with no S&P 500 member (Silver, Marine Transportation, Diversified Capital Markets, ...) is absent here and will be FLAGGED by the checker if it ever appears in the pool. That false alarm is the intended error: an omission is loud and one line to fix, while padding this list from memory would silently admit a name nobody verified. Alias TARGETS from config.universe.gics_aliases are unioned in at read time, which is how 'Coal & Consumable Fuels' (no S&P member) is already covered.",
    "refresh": "python3 -c \"import universe_source as us; s=us.UniverseSource(us.load_universe_config()); s.get_candidate_universe_detailed(); print(sorted(set(s.sp500_gics_map.values())))\" \u2014 then review the DIFF before committing: a name appearing or vanishing here is a GICS reclassification, which is a doctrine-relevant event."
  },
